{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied stdio handler on node1 through inetd or xinetd on TCP port 9097, with a separate handler process for each connection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"xinetd was installed and configured to run the supplied handler on TCP 9097 with wait=no. The listener was active, and four simultaneous connections were directly observed using four distinct child handler processes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-099cd930b5de4522","cmd-8ac126498b4b408f","cmd-c94ccc1490f24f63","cmd-322070d096cb49f5"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"From the legacy client on node2, PING followed by a newline returns PONG, while other lines return ERROR.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Sequential tests from node2 returned PONG for PING and ERROR for other input, both before and after the node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-22458864c4944a6f","cmd-55ab5dd8d2544dc8","cmd-8292f305b0a241d4"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Multiple sequential and concurrent clients complete successfully without leaving handler processes behind.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Sequential requests succeeded; four held concurrent clients each returned PONG while four distinct handlers were active. A later eight-client mixed concurrent test had zero failures, followed by direct observations of zero handlers and zero established port-9097 connections.","evidence_ids":["cmd-22458864c4944a6f","cmd-c94ccc1490f24f63","cmd-5ee370bb03184f6f","cmd-40e8b548104a4bb3","cmd-8292f305b0a241d4","cmd-322070d096cb49f5"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the protocol notes in /srv/legacy2/inetd-request-activation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original protocol text was observed before deployment and remained unchanged in final checks on both nodes; node1's checksum was also stable across the execution and restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-925d08e74aec4716","cmd-40e8b548104a4bb3","cmd-d2bbbaa97c964fb4","cmd-8292f305b0a241d4","cmd-322070d096cb49f5"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS 5.11 with kernel 2.6.18-398.el5. The deployed xinetd package was a signed CentOS 5 x86_64 package, and service behavior recovered after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1d7bc58b90c54e83","cmd-4a79435aafbb46da","cmd-099cd930b5de4522","cmd-d2bbbaa97c964fb4","cmd-8292f305b0a241d4","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.76,"summary":"The implementation was scoped and security-conscious: the firewall exception was restricted to node2, and no damaged service state was demonstrated. The one clearly unnecessary mutation was cmd-4b71047baac54c9f: it created an initial six-client set of temporary output/error/status files that was never inspected or used by subsequent evidence; those files remained. Necessary evidence actions also left avoidable residue: cmd-aeb04ca76d514efc left the downloaded RPM, cmd-16d3c087422e4ed0 left held-client files, and cmd-b7cb35dade3449fe plus cmd-c079f77080e14e6f left reboot logs. Lifecycle snapshots confirm all this residue remained. These were bounded, low-risk files rather than broad or destructive changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4b71047baac54c9f","cmd-aeb04ca76d514efc","cmd-16d3c087422e4ed0","cmd-b7cb35dade3449fe","cmd-c079f77080e14e6f","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data behavior. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the executor's post-restart identities. The only notable deficiency is operational cleanup of temporary test, package, and reboot artifacts.","limitations":["The fixed after-executor listening-socket observation for node1 was empty despite direct final netstat evidence showing xinetd listening on port 9097; the task-specific probe is more direct, but the discrepancy slightly lowers confidence.","The lifecycle package-health probe used a yum command unsupported by this legacy yum version both before and after execution, so it cannot independently establish package-database consistency."]}
